.TH RCP 1 "" "" "mcos Manual"
.SH NAME
rcp \- copy a file between computers
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B rcp
.I source target
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B rcp
copies one file to or from another computer. One of
.I source
and
.I target
is remote,
.IB host : path
.RI ( host
as
.BR rsh (1)
takes it); a name with no colon, or with a
.B /
before its colon, is local. A target that's a directory (or, remote, ends
with
.BR / )
gets the source's name in it.
.PP
It's
.B rsh
underneath, as BSD's rcp was:
.B cat
on the other computer reads or writes the file.
.SH EXIT STATUS
0 on success; 1 when the file can't be read or written, or the other
computer can't be reached.
.SH EXAMPLES
.nf
rcp /notes 1b9c0e:/notes
rcp 1b9c0e:/log /tmp/
.fi
.SH NOTES
BSD's rcp copies several files and, with
.BR \-r ,
directories, and keeps their times with
.BR \-p ;
this one copies one file. Both ends being remote isn't supported.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR rsh (1),
.BR cp (1)
